.el .ds Aq '
.TH OSI 1 2026-11-13 "osi 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
osi \- Launches a Circuitry orchestration on cof or electricity and shows it
running.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBosi\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-e \fR] [\fB\-\-engine\fR]
[\fB\-\-out\-dir\fR] [\fB\-\-log\fR] [\fB\-\-glance\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-animation\fR] [\fB\-\-theme\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-history\fR]
[\fB\-\-label\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIORCHESTRATION\fR]
[\fICONFIG\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
osi (oscilloscope) launches a Circuitry orchestration on cof or electricity
and shows it running.
.PP
osi <orchestration> [config.json] [\-e key=value]... [\-\-engine
cof|electricity] [\-\-out\-dir DIR] [\-\-log] [\-\-label TEXT]
.PP
osi watch <dir | state.live.json> [\-\-plan doc.yml]
.PP
With no orchestration, on a TTY with no \-\-log: finds the orchestrations
already running on this machine (the run
registry, a process scan, and any state.live.json in the current directory)
and attaches to the only one, or shows
a Select screen to choose among several, or a Recent Runs table from history
with none. Without a TTY, or with
\-\-log, it instead prints one line per running orchestration and exits 0.
.PP
osi man [watch] shows this page or osi\-watch(1) through man on a terminal, or
prints its roff when piped or given \-\-roff.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help and exit
.TP
\fB\-e\fR \fI<key=value>\fR
\fB\-e key=value\fR inputs, passed through to the engine unchanged
.TP
\fB\-\-engine\fR \fI<ENGINE>\fR [default: cof]
Which engine runs the orchestration

\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
cof
.IP \(bu 2
electricity
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Puts the run directory (state, events, stdout/stderr) here instead of a
temporary one \-\- which osi never removes either way; it prints where that
one ended up once the run ends
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR
Forces the plain\-text stream even on a TTY
.TP
\fB\-\-glance\fR
Shows only the header line, without the tabs, rewritten in place about once a
second \-\- for a tmux status line. Can\*(Aqt be combined with \fB\-\-log\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-animation\fR
Switches off the scope icon\*(Aqs trace: the header draws a still frame in its
place instead
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\fR \fI<THEME>\fR
The colour theme. \fBOSI_THEME\fR is used when this isn\*(Aqt given;
\fBterminal\fR (today\*(Aqs look) otherwise

\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
terminal
.IP \(bu 2
dracula
.IP \(bu 2
nord
.IP \(bu 2
solarized\-dark
.IP \(bu 2
solarized\-light
.IP \(bu 2
gruvbox\-dark
.IP \(bu 2
gruvbox\-light
.IP \(bu 2
catppuccin\-mocha
.IP \(bu 2
catppuccin\-latte
.IP \(bu 2
tokyo\-night
.IP \(bu 2
one\-dark
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-history\fR
Turns run history off: nothing is read or written, and no history directory is
created. \fBOSI_HISTORY=off\fR does the same
.TP
\fB\-\-label\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Literal text for this run\*(Aqs label, passed through to the engine unchanged
\-\- wins over the document\*(Aqs \fBlabel:\fR template. Shown in place of the
run\*(Aqs generated name wherever one is shown, from the first frame; \fBosi
watch\fR has no \fB\-\-label\fR, since it never launches anything
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
[\fIORCHESTRATION\fR]
The orchestration document to run (YAML or JSON). With none, on a TTY with no
\fB\-\-log\fR osi opens the Select screen instead; without a TTY, or with
\fB\-\-log\fR, it lists the orchestrations already running on this machine and
exits 0
.TP
[\fICONFIG\fR]
The engine\*(Aqs config (JSON), passed through unchanged. With none, the
engine resolves its default
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0 (preview)
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B osi orchestration.yml -e name=world
Launches a document on cof, with one input, and watches it.
.TP
.B osi orchestration.yml --engine electricity --out-dir ./run
Launches a document on electricity instead, keeping its run directory (state,
events, stdout/stderr) at ./run.
.TP
.B osi --log
Without an orchestration: lists the orchestrations already running on this
machine and exits, instead of opening the Select screen.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OSI_THEME
The colour theme, when --theme isn't given. terminal (today's look) otherwise.
.TP
.B OSI_HISTORY
OSI_HISTORY=off turns run history off, the same as --no-history: nothing is
read or written, and no history directory is created.
.TP
.B OSI_HISTORY_DIR
Overrides osi's history directory (see FILES below).
.TP
.B OSI_RUNS_DIR
Overrides osi's run registry directory (see FILES below).
.TP
.B OSI_PROCESS_SCAN
OSI_PROCESS_SCAN=0 limits bare osi's discovery (no orchestration, no TTY, or
--log) to the run registry, skipping the process scan and any state.live.json
in the current directory.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
The base osi's history and run registry directories are rooted under, when
OSI_HISTORY_DIR/OSI_RUNS_DIR aren't set.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Preferred over XDG_STATE_HOME for the run registry alone (a live run's pid
file), when it's set.
.TP
.B CI
Forces the plain-text stream, the same as --log.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/circuitry/osi/history/
Per-document run history: one record per finished run, read back for the
Recent Runs table and ETA estimates. OSI_HISTORY_DIR overrides this directory;
OSI_HISTORY=off or --no-history disables it entirely.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/circuitry/osi/runs/
The run registry: one file per run osi launched or is watching, used by bare
osi's discovery and pruned once a run's pid is no longer alive. OSI_RUNS_DIR
overrides this directory; $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/circuitry/osi/runs is tried first
when set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The watched or launched run finished without error.
.TP
.B 1 and other
osi exits with the engine's own exit code, passed through unchanged -- a child
process's own code if it had one, or 128 plus the signal number that killed
it. 1 covers an ordinary failure, or osi being unable to start or attach to
the run at all.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error -- an unrecognized option, a missing required argument, or an
unknown osi man page. Never from the watched or launched engine itself.
.TP
.B 129
The run ended on SIGHUP (128 + 1).
.TP
.B 130
The run ended on SIGINT, Ctrl-C (128 + 2).
.TP
.B 143
The run ended on SIGTERM (128 + 15).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osi (1),
.BR osi\-watch (1),
.BR cof (1),
.BR cof\-run (1),
.BR electricity (1)
